               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgPlanckCMB\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgPlanckCMB', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Map of the cosmic microwave background — the oldest light we can detect (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "6006cb3a-9409-4d2a-999c-e4c74249f563",
                     "text": "ESA",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Cosmic Microwave Background\n\nThe Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) is the oldest light in the universe. It has been traveling through space for billions of years and reaches us from every direction. The image in this section is a map of that light, recorded by the Planck mission of the ESA (European Space Agency) (<htcite0>).\n\n#### It Is Not Noise — It Is a Discovery\n\nIn <htdate0>, A. A. Penzias and R. W. Wilson were testing a huge horn-shaped antenna, and its temperature remained 3.5 K (Kelvin) higher than expected (<htcite1>).\n\nAbout the Kelvin scale: 0 K is the coldest temperature possible (−273.15 °C) and water freezes near 273 K. So 3.5 K is a very small difference — yet it would not go away.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "6006cb3a-9409-4d2a-999c-e4c74249f563",
                     "text": "ESA",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "587cdba4-cefb-4df9-8514-2e50ccf88739",
                     "text": "Penzias, A. A., & Wilson, R. W.",
                     "page": "pp. 419 - 420",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1965",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1965",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: What could it be?</b><br /><br />When we talk about measuring temperature, we usually think of a thermometer. Imagine that you feel fine — no fever — but when you take your temperature, the thermometer reads 40 °C.<br /><br />What would you do in that situation?<br /><br />Take a moment to think before you read on.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "You would probably suspect that something is wrong with the thermometer. Penzias and Wilson did the same: at first, they suspected a problem with their own equipment.\n\nWhile looking for a measurement error, they ruled out one possible cause after another: signals absorbed from the atmosphere, problems with the electric flow in the cables (impedance), the temperature of the antenna itself, small imperfections in its structure, and even waves that came from behind it (the backlobe) (<htcite0>).\n\nWith every instrumental problem eliminated, the mysterious signal was still there. A new explanation was needed.\n\n### Confirming the Big Bang\n\nThe measurements by Penzias and Wilson could not come from any known source in the universe, such as a star or a galaxy (<htcite1>). However, their results matched the predictions in another paper, written by R. H. Dicke, P. J. E. Peebles, P. G. Roll and D. T. Wilkinson (<htcite2>; <htcite3>).\n\nAccording to Dicke and his colleagues, the early universe started at about 10 billion Kelvin (10,000,000,000 K) and, as space expanded, cooled to roughly 3.5 K — exactly the value observed by Penzias and Wilson (<htcite4>).\n\nThis discovery did two things at once: it confirmed that the Cosmic Microwave Background really exists, and it strengthened the model of a uniform and isotropic universe — that is, one with the same properties in every direction (<htcite5>; <htcite6>).\n\n**In short:** the strange signal that Penzias and Wilson could not explain is the oldest light in the universe — a faint echo of the Big Bang that has been traveling toward us for billions of years.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "587cdba4-cefb-4df9-8514-2e50ccf88739",
                     "text": "Penzias, A. A., & Wilson, R. W.",
                     "page": "pp. 420",
                     "date_time": {
